---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../utils/urls';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="Ivan Ilin's research on LLM compression, sparse fine-tuning, pipeline parallelism, and AI agent collaboration."
>
  <header class="page-hero research-hero shell">
    <div class="page-hero-content">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1>From efficient models to collaborating agents</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">
        I study how to make large language models cheaper to train, adapt, and
        deploy. More recently, I also study when teams of AI agents are guaranteed
        to make progress together. Alongside theory, I build practical
        implementations and open-source tools.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="content-section research-index">
    <div class="shell research-layout">
      <nav class="research-toc" aria-label="Research topics">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <a href="#model-compression">Model compression</a>
        <a href="#agent-collaboration">AI agent collaboration</a>
        <a href="#pipeline-parallelism">Pipeline parallelism</a>
        <a href="#sparse-fine-tuning">Sparse fine-tuning</a>
      </nav>

      <div>
        <section class="research-chapter" id="model-compression">
          <p class="eyebrow">01 · Compression</p>
          <h2>Model compression</h2>
          <p>
            Pruning removes model weights that contribute less to a chosen objective.
            The difficult part is not producing zeros; it is deciding what to remove
            and accounting for the combined effect. My work couples queries and keys
            when choosing attention pruning masks, and uses second-order information
            to compensate for removals block by block.
          </p>
          <p>
            Quantization represents model values with fewer bits, reducing storage and
            memory traffic at the cost of approximation error. I study how that error
            propagates through the model and how to fine-tune heavily compressed
            models without relying only on the straight-through estimator.
          </p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a class="related-link" href={sitePath('/projects/qk-wanda/')}>
              <span>Project</span><strong>Pruning LLMs with QK-Wanda</strong>
            </a>
            <a class="related-link" href={sitePath('/projects/thanos/')}>
              <span>Project</span><strong>Pruning LLMs with Thanos</strong>
            </a>
            <a class="related-link" href="https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.543/">
              <span>Paper · NAACL 2025</span><strong>HIGGS: Pushing the Limits of LLM Quantization</strong>
            </a>
            <a class="related-link" href="https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/091166620a04a289c555f411d8899049-Abstract-Conference.html">
              <span>Paper · NeurIPS 2024</span><strong>PV-Tuning: Beyond Straight-Through Estimation</strong>
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="research-chapter" id="agent-collaboration">
          <p class="eyebrow">02 · Multi-agent systems</p>
          <h2>AI agent collaboration</h2>
          <p>
            When a task is too large for one agent, it is split into parts, such as
            the modules of a program or groups of robots. Each part becomes easier,
            but the parts are coupled: an agent can improve its own part while
            breaking someone else's. I model agent teams as block optimization, where
            each agent searches its own block with a correction for the others and a
            coordinator merges only verified progress, and prove when local work adds
            up to global progress.
          </p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a class="related-link" href={sitePath('/projects/local-work-global-progress/')}>
              <span>Project</span><strong>Convergence Theory of AI Agent Collaboration</strong>
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="research-chapter" id="pipeline-parallelism">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 · Distributed training</p>
          <h2>Pipeline parallelism</h2>
          <p>
            Pipeline parallelism divides a model into stages that can work on
            different microbatches concurrently. The resulting delays make its
            optimization dynamics different from ordinary synchronous training. I
            study theoretical models that make these delayed updates explicit and
            allow convergence to be analyzed.
          </p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a class="related-link" href={sitePath('/projects/pipeline-parallelism-theory/')}>
              <span>Project</span><strong>First Theory for PipeDream</strong>
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="research-chapter" id="sparse-fine-tuning">
          <p class="eyebrow">04 · Adaptation</p>
          <h2>Sparse fine-tuning</h2>
          <p>
            Sparse fine-tuning updates a selected subset of model parameters while
            keeping the rest fixed. This turns parameter selection into part of the
            optimization problem and can reduce the amount of trainable state needed
            to adapt a model.
          </p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a class="related-link" href={sitePath('/projects/sparse-fine-tuning/')}>
              <span>Project</span><strong>Super-Tuning for LLMs</strong>
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
